!!id:"https://tson.io/2026/35/ltr8/cli/diagnostics-11.tn"
!!meta:"https://tson.io/2026/35/m/meta.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/35/m/core.tn"
@doc:"""
  This project's own schema !!id convention (see CLAUDE.md's "Project-owned schema !!id
  convention"): /2026/35 is the spec revision this schema is written against, ltr8 is the
  publishing org, cli is this module's own schema group, and the trailing -11 is this schema's
  own version.

  That version is bumped on a *release*, not on a change. [TSON-SCHEMA] §10's immutability rule
  binds a published identity: once a release of this library ships carrying this schema, the
  document under this !!id is fixed, and a later shape change mints -12 rather than editing it.
  Between releases -- while the build version carries -SNAPSHOT -- nothing has published this
  identity, so this document is in development and is edited in place. Bumping per change instead
  mints versions nobody ever consumed, one for every field added during a development cycle.

  diagnostic_code, outcome and restriction_level are hand-written copies of Diagnostic.Code,
  Outcome and TsonUnicodePolicy.Level. DiagnosticsSchemaTest checks every copy against the Java enum
  in both directions, which is what catches a member added to one and forgotten here -- before that
  test existed, two such omissions were noticed only by a version bump.

  This enum copies Diagnostic.Code whole, not the subset one consumer happens to emit -- the same
  reason DUPLICATE_FIELD's absence was a defect rather than an economy.
  """
{
  diagnostic_code => !enum [
    FIELD_REQUIRED FIELD_FIXED TYPE_MISMATCH WRONG_ARITY UNKNOWN_TYPE_REF ATOM_CONSTRAINT_VIOLATION
    UNRECOGNIZED_FIELD DUPLICATE_MAP_KEY DUPLICATE_FIELD CONFUSABLE_NAMES RESTRICTED_CHARACTER RESTRICTED_SCRIPT
    SCHEMA_ERROR UNKNOWN_TYPE VALIDATION_ERROR NOT_IMPLEMENTED BIND_MISMATCH LIMIT_EXCEEDED
    SCHEMA_NOT_PERMITTED SCHEMA_NOT_FOUND SCHEMA_UNREACHABLE SCHEMA_TIMEOUT SCHEMA_TOO_LARGE
  ]
  @doc:"""
    Whether a document was checked, and if it was, what checking found. Two questions that a single
    `valid` boolean carried on one bit: a document whose schema could not be obtained, or whose types
    have no Java class here, was never read at all -- reporting it as invalid asserts a verdict this
    run cannot make, and an agent reading `if (!valid)` acts on that assertion.

    NOT_CHECKED is exactly the set of diagnostic codes that are not a verdict (Diagnostic.Code.verdict):
    the five SCHEMA_ codes, BIND_MISMATCH, NOT_IMPLEMENTED and LIMIT_EXCEEDED. A name-hygiene refusal is INVALID --
    the processor looked and declined, and the sender holds the fix.
    """
  outcome => !enum [ VALID INVALID NOT_CHECKED ]
  @doc:"""
    One problem, located at up to two ends: the value in the data (path, data_position) and the rule
    in the schema (schema_id, schema_pointer, schema_position). Either end may be absent -- a problem
    found in a schema has no data, and a schemaless read has no schema.

    Absence is written as absence: every field that may have nothing to say is optional here and
    renders as null in --output json. That includes both RFC 6901 pointers, and the distinction they
    draw is load-bearing: a present "" is the root, which a document-level schema problem genuinely
    carries, while null means this diagnostic has no such end at all.

    A position is "line:column:byte_offset", all three 1-based except byte_offset, which counts
    UTF-8 bytes from 0 ([TSON-DATA] §8.1).

    A §8.2 name-hygiene refusal is one of these like any other problem, told apart by its code --
    CONFUSABLE_NAMES for two names in one scope that read alike, RESTRICTED_CHARACTER for a character
    outside the identifier profile, RESTRICTED_SCRIPT for a script the restriction level does not admit
    (usually a combination, but at the ASCII-only level a single script with nothing mixed, which is why
    the code names what was refused rather than a mix). It carries no field of its own: what §8.2 requires
    a refusal to name -- the Unicode data version it was computed against -- is a fact about the
    processor rather than about the problem, constant for every refusal in the run, so the envelope
    states it once in `policy` and this states the remedy.
    """
  diagnostic => {
    path: text?
    schema_pointer: text?
    schema_id: text?
    code: diagnostic_code
    message: text
    expected: text?
    actual: text?
    data_position: text?
    schema_position: text?
  }
  @doc:"""
    UTS #39 §5.2's restriction levels, a hand-written copy of TsonUnicodePolicy.Level checked against it
    by DiagnosticsSchemaTest the way diagnostic_code and outcome are.
    """
  restriction_level => !enum [
    ASCII_ONLY SINGLE_SCRIPT HIGHLY_RESTRICTIVE MODERATELY_RESTRICTIVE MINIMALLY_RESTRICTIVE UNRESTRICTED
  ]
  @doc:"""
    One [TSON-DATA] §8.2 policy surface: the restriction level, whether it applies to each _/- delimited
    segment rather than to the whole text, and any script combinations admitted over and above the level
    (UTS #39 §5.2's own device for Latn+Jpan and its siblings). Each entry in `permitting` is one such
    combination, named by java.lang.Character.UnicodeScript -- Latin, Cyrillic, Han.
    """
  unicode_policy => { level: restriction_level  per_segment: boolean  permitting: [[text]] }
  @doc:"""
    What this processor's configuration does to a document's fate, stated once per run.

    §8.2's three name-hygiene rules read data the Unicode Consortium does not freeze, at a level the
    reading deployment chooses, so the same bytes may be accepted here and refused elsewhere. This is the
    statement of why: identifier_policy governs declared names, field names, type-refs and annotation
    names, where all three rules apply; token_policy governs values, where only the restricted-script rule
    can, a token having no identifier profile and no scope to be distinct within. Both keep the names
    TsonConfig gives them, so a deployment's configuration and the report it produces are one vocabulary.

    unicode_data_version is the UCD release whose tables the rules were computed against, which is the
    version §8.2's "UTS #39 data version" means (SPEC-FEEDBACK.md #6). It is here rather than on each
    diagnostic because it is constant for the life of the process, and because what a sender needs in
    order not to be refused is this record *before* it writes a document -- which `tson policy` prints
    with no document in hand.
    """
  @doc:"""
    [TSON-DATA] §9.1's resource limits: what this processor will spend reading one document, as against
    what it will admit as a name. Only nesting depth is bounded so far -- §9.1 names four other limits and
    omits the ones that bound shape rather than size (SPEC-FEEDBACK.md #33), and each lands here as it is
    built.

    A limit refusal is LIMIT_EXCEEDED and is not a verdict: the document may be well-formed, valid, and
    read in full by the next processor along. It is here for the same reason the Unicode policy is -- the
    bound is this deployment's own choice, so a sender that reads it writes a document that fits.
    """
  limits => { max_depth: int32 }

  policy => {
    identifier_policy: unicode_policy
    token_policy: unicode_policy
    unicode_data_version: text
    limits: limits
  }
  validation_report => { outcome: outcome  policy: policy  errors: [diagnostic] }
  file_report => { file: text  outcome: outcome  errors: [diagnostic] }
  validation_run => { outcome: outcome  policy: policy  files: [file_report]  errors: [diagnostic] }
}
